[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A commentary remarks on the canon; whether that is a fifth family is open

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-COMMENTARY-IS-THE-FIFTH-FAMILY
TITLE: A commentary remarks on the canon; whether that is a fifth family is open
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: The operator rules on the family, or commentary nodes prove to be a second backlog rather than a checked overlay.
STATEMENT: >>>
COMMENTARY is a remark ABOUT the canon rather than about the system the canon
describes: a gap, an architecture note, a verdict on a node, a note on one
edge. The element exists, `dev/scripts/commentary-check.py` gates it, and
NOTHING IN THE CORPUS IS ONE YET.

**What is open is the family, not the element.** [LINK: DEC-NODE-FAMILIES]
derives the vocabulary from four families and says of the third that "a
representation is never itself a claim". Read strictly, a commentary asserts
something and therefore cannot be Representation, and Commentary is a fifth
family -- which means superseding an accepted decision that sixteen narratives
cite. Read loosely, it is the odd member of Representation and nothing needs
superseding. No code reads the family, so the element is usable either way and
the ruling costs nothing to defer.

**What this does NOT retire.** The row bracket -- a `- ` line ending in
`[WORD]` or `[WORD: by]` -- stays legal, and [LINK: DEC-ROW-REFERENCE-LINES]
stays in force. 494 rows are drawn through it today, and a session that reads
"commentary replaces the row DSL" and deletes the parser takes the whole
Topics tab with it. Commentary is what a NEW remark should be; it is not a
migration of what exists.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The operator: "i dont like general NARRATIVE nodes used to document things like
gaps or other arch notes. i think this should be a new class of core cannon
grammar for effectively commentary nodes I can run semantic validators against.
i like this as an overlay for effectively edge annotations. i can disolve the
cards not rely on a meta DSL invented to fill it."

The meta DSL is concrete: a narrative with `WIDGET: rows` carries its verdicts
as a bracket at the end of a line, parsed by BRACKET_RE in view-check.py. A
validator cannot ask that structure whether a gap names what would close it,
because the whole row is one string. An element can require CLOSES_ON, and the
grammar itself enforces it.

Edge annotation is the part with no precedent, and it is why EDGE exists as a
string rather than a relation. A strictdoc relation is a TYPE/VALUE/ROLE triple
INSIDE a node with no identity of its own -- there is nothing to point a UID at.
So an edge is named structurally, and both its endpoints are ALSO written as
Remarks_On relations, which is what makes the parser refuse a dangling end: the
redundancy buys parser-enforced existence for a field the parser cannot read.
The same bargain PARENT_FP already makes.

Landing the element with zero nodes is deliberate. Every ordering for retyping
the five existing gap narratives was measured and each one loses a gap SILENTLY
-- retype-and-keep-Contains fires a finding and the walk continues, so the gap
disappears from the tab with a green build. The Gaps tab is currently the one
screen already meeting the aspiration. Breaking it to prove a mechanism is the
wrong trade.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Filed 2026-08-31. The element and its CI gate ship together because INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE forbids the other order; the check carries its own negative fixture, because a green over zero commentary nodes is otherwise indistinguishable from a checker that always returns 0.
<<<
